# What the synchronous and asynchronous node file modules agree on: the open-mode bits, spelled once.
#
# Every backend's `open` takes the same five independent booleans (read, write, create, append, clear) rather than
# a mode string, because "w+" is a node spelling and means nothing to tokio, NIOFileSystem or java.nio. This is
# where those five turn into the one thing a platform actually wants. On node that IS a mode string, folded inside
# the `grip` shim, which is the only place in the stdlib one appears.
#
# This module used to declare a SECOND `form file` beside the public one in file/handle.tree, and the two mill
# trees the ten mode-string `open` overloads were generated from. Both are superseded.
#
# Internal: reached only through the public file API.

# the open modes, as the names the public API and the tests use for them
host mode-read, text <read>
host mode-write, text <write>
host mode-create, text <create>
host mode-append, text <append>
host mode-clear, text <clear>

# where `seek` counts from: the start of the file, the cursor, or back from the end
host frame-start, text <start>
host frame-relative, text <relative>
host frame-end, text <end>
